#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ede6800b-ecfd-5ef8-a386-4aec94ddd89c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/id #uuid "f42903c2-7486-5c9a-9cc6-77437a1ea772" :question/math-academy-id "e-8483" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/7b4d47224f7078b77cef9c2893fcbe41.png)\n\n1. $y' = (x+1)(x-3)$\n2. $y'= (x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$\n3. $y' = x+1$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/e96f55679cd970ecfefdac330a9bf7e0.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -1$ and $x = 3$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y'= (x+1)(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3$.\n- The equation $y'=(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3$, but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2$, which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' = x+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=-1$ and $x=3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5277f56d-3f2d-505c-bf73-48207a2310a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/id #uuid "19f645f4-d659-514b-8ee4-369593645953" :question/math-academy-id "e-8484" :question/problem "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/c8002ac8b196f968030864b2cd40eb74.png)\n\n1. $y' = -x(x+4)^2$\n2. $y' = -x^2(x+2)$\n3. $y' = x^2(x+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/9901d4ef96057b2693867474777d9aff.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -4$ and $x = 0$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.Of the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n$y^{′}|=-x(x + 4)^{2}; y^{′}|= x^{2}(x + 4)$\n- Second, the lines $x=-4$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$x < -4$$-4 < x < 0$$x > 0$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y' = -x(x+4)^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny'= -x(x+4)^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32b9a92b-880b-50de-b8e9-648fa219b83f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/id #uuid "adcbbd25-f9e0-5afd-886a-d3ed5fef714c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8486" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/6d66b381562050b8d132c484ac1fa429.png)\n\n1. $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$\n2. $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/210333f72c3deff33c57c265f4a7f39c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-2$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $x \\neq -2$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2$, and $y'\\to 0$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{2x}{x-2} = \\dfrac{2}{\\left(1-\\dfrac2x\\right)}\\to 2 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad x\\to \\pm\\infty$.\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "879a7719-f73c-5eb6-a5b0-837446852eef" :question/math-academy-id "e-9312" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/57973b45a55a252ab3207ceb63d783b2.png)\n\n1. $y' =x^2 - 4$\n2. $y' = x^2 - 4x + 4$\n3. $y' = 4 - x^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/f688b03d6348fc4ba801b5e382ce2865.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:Region$x < -2$$-2 < x < 2$$x > 2$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$Slope$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n$y' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)$\ngives the required behavior.\n- The equation\n$y' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2$\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$, but not at $x=-2$.\n- The equation\n$y' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)$\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.Region$x < -2$$-2 < x < 2$$x > 2$Slope$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5277f56d-3f2d-505c-bf73-48207a2310a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3797386-786d-51d1-be7e-fbd72efaae2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165901} {:answer/id #uuid "c28197d2-ccad-5450-9582-c9573173f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165902} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a76cb4-7aef-5a80-99ef-a5f0b703987d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165903} {:answer/id #uuid "2359bca2-d9d7-56fb-a139-8659e97a4a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165904} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2bfea-a031-5c8e-a1ab-4f557a0b2508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c28197d2-ccad-5450-9582-c9573173f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165902} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e02a18-7868-58d5-bdaf-1adfd88958e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165900}] :question/id #uuid "e680b662-be78-5267-8eb2-c851df5a46ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-96094" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3219/Source/Images/q-96094.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x - 2$\n2. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)^{2}$\n3. $y^{′} = x(x - 2)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5277f56d-3f2d-505c-bf73-48207a2310a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69ad58a-1e63-56bd-aca0-dae7a8284167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165907} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6a9fa8-e42c-58b7-9b85-2f955c26e1b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165908} {:answer/id #uuid "465a22a5-332a-573c-83f9-3155e77b1142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165909} {:answer/id #uuid "5309a6ed-5a7d-5ad2-90d3-9bcf16cf7e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 1)(x + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165910} {:answer/id #uuid "72590c70-3e86-55c0-9088-e550779a8c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} 